PROMPT_CASE_BEGIN explain model=glm-02/glm-5.3 prompt="Read src/limit.rs and explain what first_lines actually returns and what happens for short inputs. Do not change files. Answer in English."
PROMPT_CASE_RUN explain status=Completed error=None result=Some("`first_lines` in `src/limit.rs` does the following:\n\n**What it returns**\n\n- It maps over the range `0..=MAX_LINES` — that's the **inclusive** range, so it visits indices `0, 1, 2, 3`.\n- Despite `MAX_LINES` being `3` (which reads like \"at most 3 lines\"), the function actually returns **4 elements**: `lines[0]` through `lines[3]`, each cloned into an owned `String`, collected into a `Vec<String>`.\n- So the off-by-one is real: the name suggests a cap of 3 lines, but the `..=` makes it 4. An exclusive `0..MAX_LINES` (or `..=MAX_LINES - 1`) would match the constant's intent.\n\n**Short inputs**\n\n- There is **no length check**. Indexing `lines[i]` directly means any slice shorter than 4 elements panics at runtime:\n  - `len() == 3` → panics at `i == 3`\n  - `len() == 2` → panics at `i == 2`\n  - `len() == 1` → panics at `i == 1`\n  - empty slice → panics at `i == 0`\n- The panic is the standard `index out of bounds: the len is N but the index is i` — not a graceful truncation or `None`/shorter `Vec`.\n- Only inputs with **at least 4 lines** are safe; exactly-4-line slices work and return all 4.\n\nIn short: it returns a 4-element `Vec<String>` regardless of intent, and any input shorter than 4 elements panics rather than returning the available lines. No files were changed.")
